And no, it’s not going to take your job. At least it’s not trying to. 

Entering the conversation on the role of AI tools in creative work feels somewhat like dropping into a warzone. But I’ll bite.

As a designer-founder (both startups and ad/design studios) I’ve been using AI daily since ChatGPT launched. I got obsessed when it did and even went through my own sped up version of AI psychosis during the first two weeks. Today AI tools take up 80% of my total screen time. Apparently I spent 6 hours 47 minutes in Terminal using Claude Code just yesterday. You could try saying I’m LLM-pilled and you’d be right in some ways – indeed I am more optimistic about the various tech making up what we now call AI than a lot of my peers in creative industries. But you’d be wrong in other, profound ways. 

For starters, I think that as a technology, LLMs are a dead end. Both in terms of the gold rush race to AGI and for practical applications. They will learn to write like normal humans and to reason better. They will stop pasting private keys in open chat windows (even after you ask then 10 times to not do it and write 100 internal memories about it). They will become better and better for a variety of use-cases, some of which they can barely cover today. But there will be use-cases LLMs will never be good enough or at least, not the best tool for. One of these is visual reasoning. No matter how many harnesses I write to make my Claude not store a summary of the screenshot I send it and instead burn 30x more tokens to actually look at the original image every time we discuss it, it thinks in text. It’s very difficult to force it think spatially and I know, because I fight this battle for most of the day, every day. 

The other thing that I disagree with on a visceral level (get me drunk and you’ll know) is the replacement narrative. 


And there are things that are generally worth automating. Some tasks shoudn’t exist at all. Imagine the world where the the only people that have to both write and read all the “Hope this email finds you well” and “My kindest regards” are non-people. All you need to do is look at something some shit your boss has sent you and write “Oh, that’s shit” in response, knowing that the “communicate properly” bit is covered by the AI layers between you – the enrichment+send layer on your end and the interpretation layer on theirs. Dunno about you but I’m down for this shit.
